| File: example.sdl     -- Simple prototype board, Hex file
{
|	Port_Num	Port_Type
STRING	3		HEX_FILE
|

| 	Loc.	Ref	Device		JEDEC File
DEVICE	0	U3	EPX780QC132	right.jed
DEVICE	1	U4	EPX780LC84  	left.jed
DEVICE	2	U1	PENTIUM

}  | NOTE: the braces are important!   Do not forget them!

|--------------------------------------------------------
| Other Commands to Control JED2JTAG Operations
|
| To create an example.HEX file, which can be programmed
| into a FLASH EPROM:
|	STRING 3  HEX_FILE

| To create only an example.BIN file:
|	STRING 3  BIN_FILE
|

| These commands are used when the chain is similar to that
| used by the FLEXlogic Prototyping Cable Kit, and can be hooked
| up to the computer's parallel port.
|
| To reconfigure a chain for different LPTx: ports:
|	STRING 1  PARALLEL_PORT  LPT1:
|	STRING 2  PARALLEL_PORT  LPT2:
|	STRING 2  PARALLEL_PORT  LPT3:
|	STRING 1  PARALLEL_PORT  LPT2:

| This version of PENGN and JED2JTAG talks directly to the 
| parallel port addresses.  It uses the BIOS Printer Port table 
| to determine the memory address of LPT1:, LPT2:, and LPT3:.
|
|----------------------------------------------------------
| For machines where the BIOS Printer Ports addresses
| are not stored in Address 0:408, the PORT command may be 
| inserted after the STRING command. This will configure
| the absolute Hexadecimal memory address of the LPT port.

| Example: For a machine where the BIOS Printer Port table 
| is not located at Address 0:408:
|
| 	STRING  1	PARALLEL_PORT   LPT1:
| 	PORT 03BC

| Normal LPT1:      PORT 0378
| Normal LPT2:      PORT 0278
| Compaq ProLinea:  PORT 03BC

| ------- Reference ----------------------------------------
| According to the Peter Norton PC Programmers Bible, 
| 3rd Edition, 1993 Microsoft Press.  Page 160.

| I/O Address (Hex)      Parallel Port
| -----------            -------------
| 3BC - 3BF              Port on MDA (Mono Display Adaptor)
| 378 - 37F              Second Parallel Port (378-37B on IBM